## 1. Historical Data

Add historical snapshot support for the existing hierarchy:

Tenant/Application → Namespace → Deployment → Pod

Only successful SCHEDULED auto-refresh runs should create historical snapshots.

Manual refresh must:
- update current state
- NOT create historical snapshots
- NOT run the Insight Engine

Scheduled auto-refresh must:
1. collect current data
2. update current state
3. create one coherent historical snapshot
4. run the Insight Engine
5. update active/resolved insights

Use a collection/run identifier so all records from one scheduled refresh represent the same snapshot.

Retain 30 days of detailed history.

Do not assume a fixed refresh interval in trend calculations; use timestamps.

## 2. Insight Analysis

Build modular analyzers for:

- Tenant utilization
- Namespace utilization/resource concentration
- Deployment health
- Pod health
- Restart behavior
- CrashLoopBackOff / ImagePullBackOff / Pending / Failed / OOMKilled
- Replica availability
- Utilization trends
- Historical baseline deviation
- Persistence/duration
- Rate of change
- Cross-signal correlation

The engine must derive meaningful findings rather than simply repeat existing table values.

Examples of useful findings:

"CLMAPPS utilization increased from 48% to 71% over the last two hours."

"CLMAPPS is currently 31% above its recent utilization baseline."

"payments namespace accounts for 68% of CLMAPPS resource utilization."

"payment-service has remained in CrashLoopBackOff for 2h 15m."

"order-service has remained below its desired replica count for 42 minutes."

"order-service restarted 18 times in the last hour."

"CLMAPPS utilization is rising while 4 pods are currently unhealthy."

Do not generate an insight when the available data does not support the claim.

## 3. Historical / Statistical Logic

Implement explainable statistical analysis using appropriate lightweight techniques such as:

- mean/median
- min/max
- percentage change
- rate of change
- percentiles where useful
- historical baseline
- deviation from baseline
- persistence/duration
- peer comparison
- robust anomaly detection where appropriate

The engine must become more useful as historical data accumulates.

Do not make anomaly/baseline claims when insufficient history exists.

Do not use simple thresholds as the only source of insights.

## 4. Insight Aggregator

Create a dedicated Insight Aggregator after the individual analyzers.

Pipeline:

Current State + Historical Data
        ↓
Individual Analyzers
        ↓
Candidate Findings
        ↓
Insight Aggregator
        ↓
Deduplication
        ↓
Severity/Priority Scoring
        ↓
Top 2–5 Insights
        ↓
Dashboard

The aggregator should combine related signals into a single higher-value insight where appropriate.

For example:

High utilization
+ rapidly increasing utilization
+ unhealthy pods

should produce one meaningful correlated insight rather than three unrelated messages.

Score/rank candidates using factors such as:
- severity
- historical deviation
- rate of change
- persistence
- duration
- peer difference
- cross-signal impact

Do not expose a meaningless numerical "AI score" to users unless it has a clear interpretation.

## 5. Insight Lifecycle

Persist insight state so repeated refreshes do not create duplicate insights.

Support:

NEW → ACTIVE → RESOLVED

Track appropriate metadata such as:
- entity
- entity type
- first seen
- last observed
- status
- status_since
- occurrence count
- resolution time
- relevant supporting metrics

A continuing problem must remain one active insight.

When the underlying condition disappears, mark it RESOLVED.

## 6. Historical Aggregation

Design the data model so long-term aggregation is supported.

For older numeric historical data, support daily aggregates containing at minimum:

- date
- average
- minimum
- maximum
- sample count

Do not aggregate categorical pod states using numeric averages. Use appropriate status/event aggregation for those.

Implement the aggregation logic as part of this work so the system is ready for long-term history.

